        dict(title="Download and extract this week's kit", text="Use the download button below. A ZIP is a compressed folder: extract it before editing or running anything.\n\nCreate a folder named Forge Work in Documents (or another place you can find). On Windows, right-click the downloaded ZIP and choose Extract All. On macOS, double-click it. On Linux, use your file manager's Extract option. Put the extracted files in a week folder inside Forge Work. For week 1, name it week-01.\n\nOpen that week folder. You should see README.md, core.py, main.py, test_core.py, and cases.json together. If they are inside another folder, open that inner folder in the next step. Keep each week's files in its own folder.", download=True),
        dict(title="Open the folder and a terminal", text="In VS Code, choose File → Open Folder and select the folder containing core.py. On macOS the menu may say Open instead. If asked about trusting files, review the folder and trust your own downloaded Forge kit.\n\nThe Explorer on the left should list core.py and test_core.py. Choose Terminal → New Terminal. The terminal is the panel where you type commands; Python code goes in .py files in the editor.\n\nType the command below in the terminal and press Enter. Do not type a prompt such as PS C:\\...>. You should see a Python 3 version number.", windows="py --version", mac="python3 --version", linux="python3 --version", after="On Windows, if py is not recognized but python --version works, use python instead of py when creating the environment in step 4. If neither works, finish installing Python and close and reopen VS Code. If Python opens a store instead of printing a version, install it from python.org and reopen VS Code."),
        dict(title="Give this lab its own Python environment", text="An environment is a folder named .venv that keeps this project's Python tools together. Your program files stay beside it, outside .venv.\n\nRun the first command and wait for the terminal prompt to return; creating the environment may print nothing. Then run the second command. You should see a Python 3 version number again.", windows="py -m venv .venv\n.\\.venv\\Scripts\\python.exe --version", mac="python3 -m venv .venv\n./.venv/bin/python --version", linux="python3 -m venv .venv\n./.venv/bin/python --version", after="The commands on this page call the environment's Python directly. You do not need to activate it or change PowerShell's execution policy. In VS Code, open the Command Palette (Ctrl+Shift+P on Windows/Linux, Cmd+Shift+P on macOS), choose Python: Select Interpreter, and select this folder's .venv. If it is not listed, choose Enter interpreter path and browse to the Python executable used above."),
        dict(title="Run one file before doing the lab", text="In VS Code's Explorer, click New File and name it hello.py. Type the code below in that file, then save it (Ctrl+S, or Cmd+S on macOS). This is a tool check, separate from your lab exercise.", code='print("My computer can run Python.")', windows=".\\.venv\\Scripts\\python.exe hello.py", mac="./.venv/bin/python hello.py", linux="./.venv/bin/python hello.py", after="Run the command in the terminal. Success means the terminal prints My computer can run Python. If you see that sentence, your editor, file, terminal, and interpreter are working together. You can keep hello.py as a reminder."),
        dict(title="Run the supplied tests and read the result", text="A test calls a function and compares its result with an expected value. The kit's tests check the checkpoint function in core.py. Save your files, then run:", windows=".\\.venv\\Scripts\\python.exe -m unittest -v", mac="./.venv/bin/python -m unittest -v", linux="./.venv/bin/python -m unittest -v", after="FAILED with assertion failures is expected before you implement core.py. It means Python found and ran the tests, but the unfinished function did not produce the expected result. Read the first failure: which input was used, and which two values differed?\n\nAn ERROR importing core or a missing-file message means setup needs attention; see the help below. Ran 0 tests is not success: check that the open folder contains test_core.py. OK means the tests that ran passed. It does not verify the whole lab or every possible input."),
        dict(title="Build a small piece yourself", text="Return to the lab brief. First write an example input and the output you want. Explain the steps in ordinary words. Then implement one small part, save the file, and rerun the tests.\n\ncore.py holds calculations and rules. main.py is the starting place for the complete program: call your functions, display results, and later read input. test_core.py is where you add tests of your own. cases.json supplies the existing test inputs; changing it to make a failure disappear does not fix your code. README.md describes the task and is where you record how to run your work.\n\nTo use one of your core.py functions from main.py, put an import at the top of main.py. For example, if you named a function describe_weather in core.py, the import is from core import describe_weather. Use your actual function name, not this example name. You can then call that function in main.py and print its returned value. The import names the file without .py. Keep both files in the same folder.\n\nAn import makes a definition available; it does not call the function. Read the existing from core import line in test_core.py to see the same idea in the supplied tests.\n\nWhen you have added program behavior to main.py, run it with the command below. The untouched starter only prints a reminder; that is not a finished lab.", windows=".\\.venv\\Scripts\\python.exe main.py", mac="./.venv/bin/python main.py", linux="./.venv/bin/python main.py"),
        dict(title="Write a test of your own", text="Open test_core.py. The supplied file already imports unittest, imports the checkpoint function from core, and defines a CoreTests class. A class groups the test methods; you do not need to design your own class yet.\n\nAdd the example below inside CoreTests, after the existing test method and before the final if line. Keep four spaces before def and eight before self.assertEqual. Save, then rerun the test command from step 6. The new test should appear by name.\n\nA test method name starts with test_. self is how the method accesses unittest's checking tools. assertEqual(actual, expected) compares two values and fails if they differ. This example checks Python's len function only to demonstrate the structure; it is not evidence that your lab works.", codeLabel="In test_core.py, inside CoreTests:", code='    def test_word_length(self):\n        self.assertEqual(len("owl"), 3)', after="Now add a test for a function you wrote: choose an input, calculate its expected result by hand, call the function, and compare the result with assertEqual. If it is a new function, add its name to the from core import line at the top of the file. Name the test for the behavior, such as test_exact_cutoff.\n\nTemporarily change the expected result of your new test to an incorrect value and rerun. Confirm that this test fails, then restore the correct value. That proves the test is being discovered and checks what you think it checks. Keep the supplied tests intact."),
        dict(title="Check the full program and keep your work", text="Passing the supplied tests finishes only the checkpoint function. Follow the lab's build steps, add your own tests, and try the actual program with ordinary and boundary inputs. Compare the result with each acceptance criterion.\n\nIn your README, record the run command, one example input and output, and anything unfinished. Back up your week folder to a place you control. Forge's progress export saves browser notes and code; it does not include files on your computer.\n\nBack in Forge, check only the criteria you have tried and write a short evidence note: what you ran, what happened, one problem you fixed, and any help you used. You can then continue to the next week. Git is useful for keeping file history, but you can learn it after getting your first program running."),
